<!--
  L5-06, 2026-08-31. Every live garment page, so a crawler is told they exist
  rather than having to find them.

  ⚠️ UPDATED 2026-09-04: this file listed 10 URLs covering TWO products while
  ELEVEN were live — 45 of 55 pages were absent. That is not cosmetic. This viewer
  is a single-page app with NO links between garment pages, so a crawler that
  follows links finds nothing; this file is the only way those pages are
  discoverable at all. The 45 missing ones were reachable only by someone sharing
  the link. Found by the 2026-09-04 product-page audit.

  ⚠️ UPDATED 2026-09-07: 55 -> 80. Five more garments went live that day (r-cch,
  r-gtd, r-au, r-ect, r-et), and this file was regenerated in the same change —
  which is the whole point of the 2026-09-04 note above. The catalogue growing is
  what silently narrows this file; a rename is not.

  The 80 entries below were cross-checked against THREE independent sources that
  agreed exactly, with zero discrepancies in either direction:
    1. the live CMS payload for each of the 16 products in scripts/live-products.mjs
    2. the 80-entry manifest in apps/viewer/worker/og-cards.ts
    3. the 80 .jpg files actually present under apps/viewer/public/og/
  A mismatch between those three is the tell that this file has drifted again.

  ⚠️ THIS IS A SNAPSHOT, NOT A DERIVED FILE, and that is deliberate. Generating it
  at build time would put a network call to cms.wear-run.help on the critical path
  of every build — and a Bot Fight Mode 403 from a runner would then fail the build
  for a reason that has nothing to do with the change being built.

  ⚠️ A COLOURWAY SLUG IS PRINTED ON PHYSICAL QR TAGS, so these do not drift on their
  own; a product is added or retired deliberately. Regenerate when that happens:

    for p in $(node -e "import('./scripts/live-products.mjs').then(m=>console.log(m.LIVE_PRODUCTS.map(x=>x.slug).join(' ')))"); do
      curl -s https://cms.wear-run.help/api/public/viewer/$p \
        | python3 -c "import json,sys;d=json.load(sys.stdin);[print(d['product']['slug']+'/'+c['slug']) for c in d['colourways']]"
    done

  ⚠️ UPDATED 2026-09-28: 80 -> 200. The 24 garments processed on the owner's Mac went live
  together; their 120 pages were read back out of the CMS (each garment's colour rows, in
  order), and the same 200 match apps/viewer/worker/og-cards.ts and apps/viewer/public/og/.

  No <lastmod>: a date nobody updates is worse than no date, because a crawler
  believes it. No <priority>: Google has ignored it since 2015.
-->
